<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=crane-machine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crane</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A crane is a machine that lifts and moves loads with a boom, hoist, ropes or chains and sheaves, using levers and pulleys for mechanical advantage. The shaduf, a crane-like lever for lifting irrigation water, was used in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, but building cranes appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in the late 6th century BCE, attested by cuttings for lifting tongs and Lewis irons on temple blocks. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> added pulleys and treadwheels: a polyspastos worked by four men could lift 3,000 kg. Treadwheel cranes returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> for Gothic cathedrals and harbours, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">internal combustion engines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-motor">electric motors</a>, later powered them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crane-machine.jpg" alt="Crane" width="330" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turmdrehkran-liebherr.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>late 6th century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Greece</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The shaduf of ancient Mesopotamia is sometimes counted as the first crane, but it was a lever for lifting irrigation water.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/shaduf">Shadoof<span>c. 2200 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=crane-machine">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
